  "text": "Macha was an Irish goddess. The great capital of ancient Ulster, the northernmost of the ancient provinces of Ireland, was named Emain Macha, Twins of Macha, after her. She appeared from nowhere, or from the Otherworld, at the door of the human farmer Crunniuc and made herself at home. She helped him create bounty from the land and became pregnant by him. Like other fairy lovers, Macha set one condition on her husband: he must not brag about her. At the Assembly of Ulster Crunniuc could not stay silent when he saw the king's horses and knew his supernatural wife could beat them in a race. Boasting of her prowess, he drew the king's attention, and the king demanded proof. Macha was dragged to the Assembly, her pregnancy far advanced. The king forced the race to go on. Macha easily beat the king's swift team but went into labor at the finish line. The effort killed her. She died giving birth to her twins, a girl named Fial and a boy named Fall. As she died she cursed the men of the province. She declared that every time an enemy threatened, the warriors would become weak as women for five days and four nights, for nine generations. This curse is known as the Debility of the Ulstermen. Two other figures named Macha, who may or may not have originally been the same being, appear in Irish tradition. The first was the wife of Nemed, one of the first settlers of Ireland. In her honor the first plains of Ireland were cleared of thick forests. She died of heartbreak when she foresaw the bloodshed of the great cattle raid of Cuailnge. The third was Macha Mong Rua, Red-Haired Macha, a queen of the whole island in 377 BCE. Her father Áed Ruad shared rulership with two other kings, Díthorba and Cimbáeth. When Áed drowned and Macha sought his place, her corulers objected. So she waged war on them. She killed Díthorba, besting Cimbáeth before allying with him in marriage. She forced Díthorba's five sons to dig the earthworks now known as Navan Fort. Koch connects Macha's horselike attributes to the pagan Celtic horse-goddess Epona and to the horsewoman Rhiannon of the Welsh Mabinogi, seeing in them a pan-Celtic horse goddess.",
